{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EGd3VSvZvxvCPapLKWjtkp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RsX9papcFA25vn5JXksogm","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experiments**. Review the paper below thoroughly and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\"\n\nThe paper investigates why variable-rate imagination (Δt>1) degrades performance in DreamerV3. Through factorial experiments across 3 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop), 3 seeds each, and controlled ablations of temporal embedding, the paper establishes a causal chain: trained Δt-embedding enables the value function to exploit imprecise RSSM predictions at Δt>1, producing systematic value overestimation (\"delusional critic\") that drives policy collapse. Removing or freezing the embedding eliminates the pathology. Severity scales with RSSM prediction-quality gap between environments.\n\n**Key configurations tested:**\n- Baseline: Δt=1, standard DreamerV3\n- 008d: Subsample ON + trained Δt-emb + H=8 (collapse condition)\n- 008f: Subsample ON + dt_embed_dim=0 + H=8 (causal control)\n- 008a: Subsample OFF + untrained Δt-emb + H=15 (value suppression)\n\n**Core findings:**\n1. Trained Δt-embedding is the primary contributing factor to value overestimation collapse in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds collapse, 1/3 degradation; nve>1.5× in all seeds)\n2. Removing Δt-embedding (dt_embed_dim=0) eliminates collapse across all seeds and environments\n3. Environment-dependent modulation: Walker Walk (high RSSM gap, 1.62×) collapses; Cheetah Run (low gap, 1.31×) degrades gradually; Hopper Hop falls in between\n4. Untrained (frozen-random) Δt-embedding suppresses value growth, prevents collapse, 2/3 seeds show V-shaped recovery in Walker Walk\n5. Normalised v/e overshoot ratio proposed as diagnostic heuristic (≥1.5× threshold, environment-dependent)\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1: Walker Walk baseline calibration (3 seeds, CV<2%, v/e stable at 67-74%)\n- 4.2: 008d collapse replication (2/3 seeds collapse, all nve>1.5×)\n- 4.3: 008f stability (all nve converges to 0.68-0.82×, no collapse)\n- 4.4: 008a V-recovery (nve<1.0× all seeds, V-recovery in 2/3, mean 693 at 105K)\n- 4.5: Cheetah Run environment-dependent modulation (degradation not collapse, nve 1.47× vs Walker's >2.0×)\n- 4.6: RSSM prediction quality analysis (Walker dt2/dt1 ratio 1.62 vs Cheetah 1.31)\n- 4.7: Hopper Hop third-environment validation (nve 1.55×, 1/3 collapse)\n- 4.8: Actor entropy analysis (collapsed configs show increasing entropy, healthy configs stable/decreasing)\n- 4.9: Value calibration V(s) vs MC return (008d: correlation 0.35, bias +45%, variance ratio 2.1)\n- 4.10: Δt-embedding divergence timeline (cosine similarity drops from 0.92 to 0.42, co-occurs with nve acceleration)\n\n**Methodology (Section 3):**\n- Base: DreamerV3 with default hyperparameters on DMC\n- Variable-rate imagination: Δt-embedding e(Δt) ∈ R^d concatenated to RSSM input\n- Three embedding regimes: trained, untrained (frozen-random), removed (d=0)\n- Factorial design: 3 factors (embedding type, temporal subsampling ON/OFF, imagination horizon H=8/H=15)\n- Diagnostic metric: normalised v/e overshoot ratio = v/e_experiment(t) / v/e_baseline(t)\n\n## Review Instructions\n\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions, rating each 1-10:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the factorial experiment design appropriate? Are the ablations well-controlled? Is the causal reasoning valid? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are results statistically reliable? Are there enough seeds? Is the evaluation thorough? Are comparisons fair?\n3. **Clarity of experimental presentation**: Are tables/figures well-organized? Is it easy to follow the experimental logic?\n4. **Completeness of experiments**: Are there missing experiments that would strengthen the claims? How well does the paper cover the planned experiment set?\n5. **Soundness of the diagnostic metric (nve)**: Is the normalised v/e ratio well-motivated? Are its limitations adequately discussed?\n\nFor each dimension, provide:\n- Rating (1-10)\n- Key strengths\n- Key weaknesses\n- Specific suggestions\n\nThen provide:\n- **Overall assessment** of methodology and experiments\n- **Top 3 strengths**\n- **Top 3 weaknesses**\n- **Questions for authors**\n- **Overall score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a diagnostic/analysis paper, not an algorithm paper — judge it on the quality of its scientific investigation.","model":"opus","run_in_background":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2502,"cache_read_input_tokens":54107,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2502},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a90c8859-8e50-4bd7-a75e-c4f31c8dbc1b","uuid":"97eaf8ea-00bf-483b-8b63-dfad65d0b941"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a3e965c5956758257","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RsX9papcFA25vn5JXksogm","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experiments**. Review the paper below thoroughly and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\"\n\nThe paper investigates why variable-rate imagination (Δt>1) degrades performance in DreamerV3. Through factorial experiments across 3 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop), 3 seeds each, and controlled ablations of temporal embedding, the paper establishes a causal chain: trained Δt-embedding enables the value function to exploit imprecise RSSM predictions at Δt>1, producing systematic value overestimation (\"delusional critic\") that drives policy collapse. Removing or freezing the embedding eliminates the pathology. Severity scales with RSSM prediction-quality gap between environments.\n\n**Key configurations tested:**\n- Baseline: Δt=1, standard DreamerV3\n- 008d: Subsample ON + trained Δt-emb + H=8 (collapse condition)\n- 008f: Subsample ON + dt_embed_dim=0 + H=8 (causal control)\n- 008a: Subsample OFF + untrained Δt-emb + H=15 (value suppression)\n\n**Core findings:**\n1. Trained Δt-embedding is the primary contributing factor to value overestimation collapse in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds collapse, 1/3 degradation; nve>1.5× in all seeds)\n2. Removing Δt-embedding (dt_embed_dim=0) eliminates collapse across all seeds and environments\n3. Environment-dependent modulation: Walker Walk (high RSSM gap, 1.62×) collapses; Cheetah Run (low gap, 1.31×) degrades gradually; Hopper Hop falls in between\n4. Untrained (frozen-random) Δt-embedding suppresses value growth, prevents collapse, 2/3 seeds show V-shaped recovery in Walker Walk\n5. Normalised v/e overshoot ratio proposed as diagnostic heuristic (≥1.5× threshold, environment-dependent)\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1: Walker Walk baseline calibration (3 seeds, CV<2%, v/e stable at 67-74%)\n- 4.2: 008d collapse replication (2/3 seeds collapse, all nve>1.5×)\n- 4.3: 008f stability (all nve converges to 0.68-0.82×, no collapse)\n- 4.4: 008a V-recovery (nve<1.0× all seeds, V-recovery in 2/3, mean 693 at 105K)\n- 4.5: Cheetah Run environment-dependent modulation (degradation not collapse, nve 1.47× vs Walker's >2.0×)\n- 4.6: RSSM prediction quality analysis (Walker dt2/dt1 ratio 1.62 vs Cheetah 1.31)\n- 4.7: Hopper Hop third-environment validation (nve 1.55×, 1/3 collapse)\n- 4.8: Actor entropy analysis (collapsed configs show increasing entropy, healthy configs stable/decreasing)\n- 4.9: Value calibration V(s) vs MC return (008d: correlation 0.35, bias +45%, variance ratio 2.1)\n- 4.10: Δt-embedding divergence timeline (cosine similarity drops from 0.92 to 0.42, co-occurs with nve acceleration)\n\n**Methodology (Section 3):**\n- Base: DreamerV3 with default hyperparameters on DMC\n- Variable-rate imagination: Δt-embedding e(Δt) ∈ R^d concatenated to RSSM input\n- Three embedding regimes: trained, untrained (frozen-random), removed (d=0)\n- Factorial design: 3 factors (embedding type, temporal subsampling ON/OFF, imagination horizon H=8/H=15)\n- Diagnostic metric: normalised v/e overshoot ratio = v/e_experiment(t) / v/e_baseline(t)\n\n## Review Instructions\n\nEvaluate the paper on these dimensions, rating each 1-10:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the factorial experiment design appropriate? Are the ablations well-controlled? Is the causal reasoning valid? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are results statistically reliable? Are there enough seeds? Is the evaluation thorough? Are comparisons fair?\n3. **Clarity of experimental presentation**: Are tables/figures well-organized? Is it easy to follow the experimental logic?\n4. **Completeness of experiments**: Are there missing experiments that would strengthen the claims? How well does the paper cover the planned experiment set?\n5. **Soundness of the diagnostic metric (nve)**: Is the normalised v/e ratio well-motivated? Are its limitations adequately discussed?\n\nFor each dimension, provide:\n- Rating (1-10)\n- Key strengths\n- Key weaknesses\n- Specific suggestions\n\nThen provide:\n- **Overall assessment** of methodology and experiments\n- **Top 3 strengths**\n- **Top 3 weaknesses**\n- **Questions for authors**\n- **Overall score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a diagnostic/analysis paper, not an algorithm paper — judge it on the quality of its scientific investigation.","uuid":"9c2e4aa5-9aff-4e1d-abbb-0fae6dcf620b","session_id":"a90c8859-8e50-4bd7-a75e-c4f31c8dbc1b"}
