{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NtiJLkF8JTHkStVQ2TtCJ2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T062606\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T07:08:19.382629+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# R1 Review — Methodology & Experiments\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\"\n11\t\n12\t**Reviewer**: R1 (Methodology & Experiments focus)\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Methodological Soundness — **6/10**\n17\t\n18\t### Strengths\n19\t\n20\t- **The 008d vs 008f single-variable isolation is the paper's methodological crown jewel.** Both configurations share subsample=ON, dt=2 imagination, H=8. The only difference is dt_embed_dim (32 vs 0). This is a clean controlled experiment that directly tests the causal role of dt-embedding conditioning. The effect is large and directionally unambiguous: nve 1.71x vs 0.73x at 35K, collapse vs stability.\n21\t\n22\t- **The factorial structure is well-motivated.** The 2x2x2 design (embedding type x subsample x horizon) is an appropriate framework for disentangling confounded variables. The paper correctly identifies and addresses the confound between dt_embedding training and training data composition (subsample ON/OFF) that plagued the earlier 008c vs 008d comparison.\n23\t\n24\t- **The causal chain is constructed incrementally and each link has independent evidence.** RSSM fidelity gap (Section 4.6) -> embedding differentiation timeline (Section 4.10) -> value overestimation onset (Section 4.2) -> actor entropy rise (Section 4.8) -> collapse. This multi-link structure with independent verification at each step is rigorous.\n25\t\n26\t### Weaknesses\n27\t\n28\t- **The factorial grid has missing cells that create lingering confounds.** The smoking-gun pair (008d/008f) uses H=8, while the key positive result (008a) uses H=15 with subsample OFF. There is no trained dt_emb + H=15 + subsample OFF cell, and no dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 cell. Comparing across these groups requires the assumption that horizon and subsample effects are independent of the dt_embedding effect -- an assumption the paper itself undermines when discussing learning signal reduction (C5). The paper should explicitly present the full 2x2x2 grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify why the missing cells don't invalidate cross-group comparisons.\n29\t\n30\t- **The causal reasoning conflates \"primary contributing factor\" with \"root cause.\"** In Walker Walk, 2/3 seeds fully collapse and 1/3 shows degradation without full collapse. In Cheetah Run, 0/3 seeds collapse (only gradual degradation). This pattern is consistent with trained dt_embedding being a *contributing* factor whose severity depends on RSSM prediction quality, but the paper's language (\"causal mechanism,\" \"root cause\") implies necessity and sufficiency. The data actually show that trained dt_embedding is neither necessary for degradation (dt>1 imagination degrades performance even without it, per 008f's low absolute performance) nor always sufficient for collapse (Cheetah Run, Walker seed 2 in median scenario).\n31\t\n32\t- **The 008c vs 008d comparison still confounds two variables.** While 008f resolves the dt_embedding conditioning confound, the paper still references the 008c vs 008d comparison as supporting evidence. These two experiments differ in both dt_embedding training status AND subsample ON/OFF (hence RSSM training data composition). The paper should clearly relegate this comparison to preliminary/motivating evidence rather than treating it as a controlled experiment.\n33\t\n34\t### Suggestions\n35\t1. Present the full factorial grid explicitly, marking completed vs missing cells.\n36\t2. Replace \"root cause\" language with \"primary contributing factor\" or \"sufficient accelerant\" -- the data support the latter, not the former.\n37\t3. Clarify the logical status of each comparison: which are controlled experiments (single variable changed) vs. which are suggestive but confounded.\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 2. Experimental Rigor — **5/10**\n42\t\n43\t### Strengths\n44\t\n45\t- **The baseline calibration is thorough and provides a solid reference.** 3 seeds with CV<2% on eval return and tight v/e calibration curves (std ~0.014 on v/e ratio). This gives the normalized v/e metric a reliable denominator.\n46\t\n47\t- **The 008d collapse replicates across 2/3 seeds with consistent nve trajectories.** Seeds 0 and 1 show accelerating nve (>2.0x at 45K) with declining eval returns. The pattern is qualitatively consistent even when the third seed shows degradation without full collapse.\n48\t\n49\t- **The 008f stability is robust across all 3 seeds.** nve converges to 0.68-0.82x range with no seed showing collapse. This is the cleanest multi-seed result in the paper.\n50\t\n51\t### Weaknesses\n52\t\n53\t- **3 seeds is below the standard expected at top ML venues for RL experiments.** DreamerV3 on DMC is notoriously high-variance (especially for locomotion tasks with contact dynamics). The RL community standard for claims about \"consistent\" behavior is 5-10 seeds. With 3 seeds, the 008d result (2/3 collapse, 1/3 degradation) has a wide confidence interval -- it's equally consistent with a 40% collapse rate or a 90% collapse rate. The binary \"collapse vs no collapse\" framing relies on n=3 to claim near-certainty.\n54\t\n55\t- **The Cheetah Run results undermine the \"9/9 collapse\" headline without adequate discussion.** The paper summary claims \"2/3 seeds collapse, 1/3 degradation\" in Walker Walk 008d, yet Cheetah Run trained dt_emb shows 0/3 collapse (only degradation with nve 1.25-1.52x, borderline). This means the total collapse count across environments is actually 2/9 (or 3/9 counting Hopper's 1/3), not 9/9. The environment-dependent modulation framing (Section 4.5) is a reasonable response, but the paper needs to lead with this nuance rather than burying it.\n56\t\n57\t- **The Hopper Hop results are too thin.** Section 4.7 reports nve 1.55x and 1/3 collapse but provides no detailed per-seed tables, no learning curves, and no comparison with Hopper-specific baselines. This asymmetry in depth (10 subsections for Walker, 2 sentences for Hopper) raises questions about cherry-picking.\n58\t\n59\t- **Statistical testing is absent.** No confidence intervals, no hypothesis tests, no effect size calculations beyond informal nve comparisons. The Cohen's d claim (\">10\") is stated without the actual computation. For a paper whose central contribution is empirical diagnosis, formal statistical treatment is expected.\n60\t\n61\t### Suggestions\n62\t1. Run 5 seeds minimum for the core comparison (008d vs 008f in Walker Walk).\n63\t2. Report confidence intervals on all key metrics (nve, eval return).\n64\t3. Provide equal depth for all three environments or explicitly scope claims to Walker Walk with Cheetah/Hopper as supporting evidence.\n65\t4. Compute and report actual Cohen's d, p-values from permutation tests or Mann-Whitney U for the 008d vs 008f comparison.\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 3. Clarity of Experimental Presentation — **7/10**\n70\t\n71\t### Strengths\n72\t\n73\t- **The section structure (4.1-4.10) follows the causal chain logically.** Each subsection addresses a specific question, building from baseline calibration through collapse replication, causal control, environment generalization, and mechanistic analysis. A reader can follow the investigative logic sequentially.\n74\t\n75\t- **The normalized v/e metric is well-defined and consistently applied.** The definition (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) is introduced early (Section 3) and used uniformly throughout. The three-zone interpretation (>1.5x pathological, 0.8-1.2x normal, <0.7x suppression) provides clear anchors.\n76\t\n77\t- **The paper correctly identifies itself as diagnostic rather than algorithmic.** This sets appropriate expectations for the reader.\n78\t\n79\t### Weaknesses\n80\t\n81\t- **The experiment naming convention (008a, 008c, 008d, 008e_v3, 008f) is opaque.** These IDs carry no mnemonic information. The paper would benefit from descriptive shorthand (e.g., \"Trained-H8\", \"NoEmb-H8\", \"Untrained-H15\") used alongside or in place of the registry IDs.\n82\t\n83\t- **The relationship between Sections 4.2 and 4.5 creates confusion about the collapse claim.** Section 4.2 establishes collapse in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds), then Section 4.5 reveals Cheetah Run doesn't collapse. A reader who reaches 4.2's conclusion (\"trained dt_embedding causes collapse\") must substantially revise that understanding at 4.5 (\"...but only in Walker Walk\"). The paper should signal this environment-dependence earlier, perhaps in the introduction or at the end of Section 4.2.\n84\t\n85\t- **Tables/figures for Hopper Hop (Section 4.7) are underdeveloped relative to the other environments.** If Hopper is included, it should receive comparable treatment; if it's preliminary, it should be explicitly labeled as such or moved to supplementary material.\n86\t\n87\t### Suggestions\n88\t1. Adopt descriptive configuration names throughout.\n89\t2. Add a single summary table early (Section 3 or beginning of Section 4) showing all configurations with their key parameters and status.\n90\t3. Signal environment-dependent modulation in the introduction so Section 4.5 is expected rather than surprising.\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## 4. Completeness of Experiments — **5/10**\n95\t\n96\t### Strengths\n97\t\n98\t- **The Walker Walk experimental suite is thorough.** Sections 4.1-4.4 cover baseline calibration, collapse replication, causal control (008f), and value suppression (008a) with multi-seed data. The 008d vs 008f comparison directly addresses the central claim.\n99\t\n100\t- **The analysis experiments (Sections 4.6-4.10) add genuine mechanistic depth.** The RSSM fidelity gap analysis, actor entropy analysis, value calibration, and embedding cosine similarity each provide independent evidence for different links in the causal chain. These are not just supplementary figures -- they substantively strengthen the argument.\n101\t\n102\t### Weaknesses\n103\t\n104\t- **Missing critical experiments that would strengthen the claims:**\n105\t\n106\t  1. **Trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15**: This would test whether trained dt_embedding causes collapse even without the subsample-induced training data shift. Currently, all trained dt_emb experiments use subsample ON, so there's a residual confound between \"RSSM trained on dt=2 transitions\" and \"dt_embedding trained.\"\n107\t\n108\t  2. **dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15**: The causal control (008f) uses H=8. Would removing dt_embedding at H=15 (matching 008a's horizon) also prevent collapse? This tests whether the H=8/H=15 difference interacts with the dt_embedding effect.\n109\t\n110\t  3. **Frozen trained dt_embedding**: The paper compares trained vs untrained (random frozen) vs removed. A fourth condition -- dt_embedding trained for N steps then frozen -- would test whether the critical factor is the ongoing co-adaptation of dt_embedding and value function, or simply the existence of differentiated embeddings.\n111\t\n112\t  4. **A non-locomotion DMC environment**: All three environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) are locomotion tasks with similar dynamics structures. Testing on a manipulation or balance task would strengthen the generality claim.\n113\t\n114\t- **The Cheetah Run \"degradation not collapse\" result is under-analyzed.** The paper reports this as \"environment-dependent modulation\" (Section 4.5) and links it to RSSM prediction quality (Section 4.6), but doesn't investigate whether the degradation would eventually become collapse given more training steps. Running Cheetah 008d-equivalent to 200K+ steps would determine whether the difference is qualitative (different failure mode) or quantitative (same failure mode, delayed onset).\n115\t\n116\t- **No ablation on dt_embedding dimension.** The paper compares dim=32 vs dim=0. Is there a smooth transition? Does dim=4 or dim=8 produce intermediate overestimation? This would strengthen the mechanistic claim.\n117\t\n118\t### Suggestions\n119\t1. Prioritize the trained dt_emb + subsample OFF experiment -- it directly addresses the remaining confound.\n120\t2. Run Cheetah Run trained dt_emb to 200K+ to determine if degradation eventually becomes collapse.\n121\t3. At minimum, add the dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 cell to the factorial grid.\n122\t\n123\t---\n124\t\n125\t## 5. Soundness of the Diagnostic Metric (nve) — **6/10**\n126\t\n127\t### Strengths\n128\t\n129\t- **The normalization by baseline v/e at the same training step is elegant and necessary.** Raw v/e ratios are phase-dependent (baseline v/e goes from 9% to 70% over training), so comparing absolute v/e across time or configurations is meaningless. The normalization removes this confound.\n130\t\n131\t- **The metric correctly identifies pathological cases.** In Walker Walk, all collapsed configurations show nve >1.5x before collapse, and all stable configurations show nve <1.0x. The separation is clean in this environment.\n132\t\n133\t- **The metric is simple, cheap to compute, and interpretable.** This gives it practical value as a monitoring tool during training.\n134\t\n135\t### Weaknesses\n136\t\n137\t- **The 1.5x threshold is derived from and evaluated on the same data.** There is no independent validation. The paper reports nve values ranging from 0.55x (stable) to 2.15x (collapse) with a clean gap around 1.0-1.5x. But this gap may be an artifact of the specific configurations tested. A threshold derived post-hoc from the experimental data cannot be presented as a general diagnostic criterion without validation on held-out configurations or environments.\n138\t\n139\t- **The metric's behavior in Cheetah Run challenges its universality.** Cheetah trained dt_emb shows nve 1.25-1.52x -- borderline by the 1.5x threshold -- yet does not collapse. The paper frames this as \"environment-dependent modulation,\" which is reasonable, but it means the 1.5x threshold is not environment-agnostic despite the paper's claim. The metric correctly identifies the direction (elevated nve = concerning) but the threshold doesn't transfer.\n140\t\n141\t- **The metric conflates two distinct phenomena.** High nve can arise from (a) the value function overestimating due to model exploitation, or (b) the evaluation return being depressed by a bad policy while the value function is calibrated to an earlier, better policy. The paper doesn't distinguish these cases. In the early stages of collapse (e.g., 008d at 25K), is nve high because V is too high, or because eval_return is already dropping? Plotting V(s) and eval_return trajectories separately would resolve this ambiguity.\n142\t\n143\t- **No sensitivity analysis.** How much does the nve trajectory change if baseline v/e is computed from a different seed? The baseline v/e std is ~0.014 across seeds, which translates to ~5% nve noise. Is this enough to move a borderline case (nve 1.47x in Cheetah) above or below threshold?\n144\t\n145\t- **The metric is undefined or degenerate when baseline v/e is near zero.** At step 5K, baseline v/e is ~1% (value_mean near zero). Dividing by near-zero produces noise. The paper should specify a minimum step or minimum baseline v/e below which nve is not computed.\n146\t\n147\t### Suggestions\n148\t1. Report the actual gap between the highest stable nve and lowest collapsed nve. If the gap is [0.95, 1.52], any threshold in that range works, and the choice of 1.5x is less critical. If the gap is [1.40, 1.52], the threshold is fragile.\n149\t2. Add sensitivity analysis: how does nve change with +-1 std on the baseline v/e reference?\n150\t3. Discuss the degenerate early-training regime and specify a valid computation window.\n151\t4. Consider supplementing nve with a direct value calibration metric (V(s) vs MC return correlation, as in Section 4.9) for disambiguation.\n152\t\n153\t---\n154\t\n155\t## Overall Assessment\n156\t\n157\tThis paper identifies a genuine and specific failure mechanism -- trained dt-embedding enabling value overestimation via RSSM fidelity gaps -- and the core controlled experiment (008d vs 008f) is methodologically strong. The multi-link causal chain with independent evidence at each step represents careful scientific investigation. The normalized v/e metric, while imperfect, is a useful contribution.\n158\t\n159\tHowever, several issues collectively place the paper below the acceptance threshold in its current form:\n160\t\n161\t1. **The generality claims exceed the evidence.** The paper's strongest results come from a single environment (Walker Walk). Cheetah Run shows degradation rather than collapse, and Hopper Hop is underreported. The \"environment-dependent modulation\" framing is a reasonable interpretation but weakens the diagnostic's claimed universality.\n162\t\n163\t2. **Statistical rigor is insufficient.** 3 seeds is too few for binary collapse/non-collapse claims in RL. No formal statistical tests, no confidence intervals, no rigorous effect size reporting. The 1.5x threshold is post-hoc with no validation.\n164\t\n165\t3. **The factorial design has important missing cells.** The absence of trained dt_emb + subsample OFF and dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 leaves residual confounds that weaken the \"single variable isolation\" narrative.\n166\t\n167\t4. **No engagement with fixability.** A diagnostic paper's value depends partly on whether the diagnosis enables solutions. The paper stops at \"don't do this\" without testing any mitigation.\n168\t\n169\t---\n170\t\n171\t## Top 3 Strengths\n172\t\n173\t**S1. The 008d vs 008f controlled experiment is exemplary.** Single-variable isolation (dt_embed_dim 32 vs 0) with large effect size (nve 1.71x vs 0.73x at 35K) and consistent replication (3/3 stable vs 2/3 collapse). This is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper.\n174\t\n175\t**S2. The multi-link causal chain with independent verification is rigorous.** RSSM fidelity gap -> embedding differentiation -> value overestimation -> actor entropy rise -> collapse. Each link has its own dedicated analysis (Sections 4.6, 4.10, 4.2/4.3, 4.8, 4.9). This structure goes beyond correlation to provide genuine mechanistic understanding.\n176\t\n177\t**S3. The normalized v/e overshoot ratio is a practical diagnostic contribution.** Despite its limitations (post-hoc threshold, environment-dependent calibration), the metric is simple, interpretable, and correctly separates healthy from pathological regimes in the tested configurations. It could see adoption as a monitoring tool in MBRL temporal abstraction research.\n178\t\n179\t---\n180\t\n181\t## Top 3 Weaknesses\n182\t\n183\t**W1. Statistical underpowering and absent formal testing.** 3 seeds per configuration is insufficient for the claims made. The 008d result (2/3 collapse in Walker, 0/3 in Cheetah) has wide confidence intervals that the paper does not report. No hypothesis tests, no confidence intervals, no formally computed effect sizes. For a paper whose contribution is empirical diagnosis, this is a significant gap.\n184\t\n185\t**W2. Environment generalization is weaker than claimed.** The causal mechanism (trained dt_embedding -> delusional critic) produces collapse only in Walker Walk. In Cheetah Run, the same mechanism produces gradual degradation with borderline nve (1.47x mean). In Hopper Hop, only 1/3 seeds collapse. The 1.5x threshold and the \"environment-agnostic diagnostic\" framing are not supported by the cross-environment data.\n186\t\n187\t**W3. Incomplete factorial design leaves residual confounds.** The smoking-gun pair (008d/008f) uses H=8, while the key C3 result (008a) uses H=15 + subsample OFF. Without a trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 cell, the paper cannot rule out that the interaction of subsample ON + trained dt_emb (not trained dt_emb alone) drives collapse. Similarly, the absence of dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 means we don't know if the dt_embedding effect is horizon-dependent.\n188\t\n189\t---\n190\t\n191\t## Questions for Authors\n192\t\n193\t**Q1.** The Cheetah Run trained dt_emb configuration shows nve 1.47x at 55K (below the 1.5x threshold) with no collapse. What happens if you run this configuration to 200K steps? Does the degradation eventually become collapse (same mechanism, delayed onset) or does it plateau (qualitatively different)?\n194\t\n195\t**Q2.** What is the actual separation gap between the highest nve of any stable seed and the lowest nve of any collapsed seed across all environments? This would let us evaluate the robustness of ANY threshold, not just 1.5x.\n196\t\n197\t**Q3.** Have you tried training the dt_embedding for a fixed number of steps and then freezing it? This would test whether the ongoing co-adaptation of dt_embedding and value function is critical, or whether having differentiated (but static) embeddings is sufficient to trigger collapse. The distinction matters for mitigation strategies.\n198\t\n199\t**Q4.** The baseline v/e stabilizes at ~70%, meaning DreamerV3 systematically overestimates values even at dt=1. What mechanistically distinguishes this \"tolerable\" overestimation from the \"delusional\" overestimation at dt>1? Is there a qualitative phase transition (e.g., self-reinforcing feedback loop) or merely a matter of degree?\n200\t\n201\t**Q5.** The RSSM prediction quality analysis (Section 4.6) shows Walker dt2/dt1 ratio 1.62 vs Cheetah 1.31. With only two data points, the correlation between RSSM fidelity gap and collapse severity is suggestive but not established. Do you have the Hopper Hop RSSM fidelity ratio? If so, does it fall between Walker and Cheetah as predicted?\n202\t\n203\t**Q6.** Section 4.10 reports cosine similarity dropping from 0.92 to 0.42, co-occurring with nve acceleration. Is this measured from a single seed? What is the cross-seed variance on the cosine similarity trajectory?\n204\t\n205\t---\n206\t\n207\t## Scores\n208\t\n209\t| Dimension | Score |\n210\t|-----------|-------|\n211\t| Methodological soundness | 6/10 |\n212\t| Experimental rigor | 5/10 |\n213\t| Clarity of presentation | 7/10 |\n214\t| Completeness of experiments | 5/10 |\n215\t| Soundness of diagnostic metric | 6/10 |\n216\t\n217\t**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n218\t\n219\t**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n220\t\n221\t---\n222\t\n223\t## Path to Acceptance\n224\t\n225\tThe core scientific investigation is sound and the central experiment (008d vs 008f) is compelling. To reach acceptance:\n226\t\n227\t1. **Add 2+ seeds** to the core Walker Walk comparisons (008d, 008f) and report formal statistics.\n228\t2. **Fill the critical missing factorial cell**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15.\n229\t3. **Run Cheetah trained dt_emb to 200K+** to determine whether degradation eventually becomes collapse.\n230\t4. **Add threshold sensitivity analysis** and acknowledge that the 1.5x threshold is environment-dependent.\n231\t5. **Scope claims to match evidence**: \"RSSM-based world models\" not \"world-model RL\" generally; \"contributes to collapse in high-RSSM-gap environments\" not \"causes collapse.\"\n232\t6. **Test at least one mitigation** (e.g., conservative value penalty, frozen-after-N-steps dt_embedding) to demonstrate the diagnosis is actionable.\n233\t\n234\tIf the empirical results hold up with more seeds and the claims are properly scoped, this could be a solid 4-5/6 paper.\n235\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a90c8859-8e50-4bd7-a75e-c4f31c8dbc1b","uuid":"71d0c426-3d08-4e42-84fb-2a881fadbad0","timestamp":"2026-05-14T07:08:57.869Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T062606/median/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T062606\ntier: median\noverall_score: weak reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T07:08:19.382629+00:00\n---\n\n# R1 Review — Methodology & Experiments\n\n**Paper**: \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\"\n\n**Reviewer**: R1 (Methodology & Experiments focus)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Soundness — **6/10**\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The 008d vs 008f single-variable isolation is the paper's methodological crown jewel.** Both configurations share subsample=ON, dt=2 imagination, H=8. The only difference is dt_embed_dim (32 vs 0). This is a clean controlled experiment that directly tests the causal role of dt-embedding conditioning. The effect is large and directionally unambiguous: nve 1.71x vs 0.73x at 35K, collapse vs stability.\n\n- **The factorial structure is well-motivated.** The 2x2x2 design (embedding type x subsample x horizon) is an appropriate framework for disentangling confounded variables. The paper correctly identifies and addresses the confound between dt_embedding training and training data composition (subsample ON/OFF) that plagued the earlier 008c vs 008d comparison.\n\n- **The causal chain is constructed incrementally and each link has independent evidence.** RSSM fidelity gap (Section 4.6) -> embedding differentiation timeline (Section 4.10) -> value overestimation onset (Section 4.2) -> actor entropy rise (Section 4.8) -> collapse. This multi-link structure with independent verification at each step is rigorous.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The factorial grid has missing cells that create lingering confounds.** The smoking-gun pair (008d/008f) uses H=8, while the key positive result (008a) uses H=15 with subsample OFF. There is no trained dt_emb + H=15 + subsample OFF cell, and no dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 cell. Comparing across these groups requires the assumption that horizon and subsample effects are independent of the dt_embedding effect -- an assumption the paper itself undermines when discussing learning signal reduction (C5). The paper should explicitly present the full 2x2x2 grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify why the missing cells don't invalidate cross-group comparisons.\n\n- **The causal reasoning conflates \"primary contributing factor\" with \"root cause.\"** In Walker Walk, 2/3 seeds fully collapse and 1/3 shows degradation without full collapse. In Cheetah Run, 0/3 seeds collapse (only gradual degradation). This pattern is consistent with trained dt_embedding being a *contributing* factor whose severity depends on RSSM prediction quality, but the paper's language (\"causal mechanism,\" \"root cause\") implies necessity and sufficiency. The data actually show that trained dt_embedding is neither necessary for degradation (dt>1 imagination degrades performance even without it, per 008f's low absolute performance) nor always sufficient for collapse (Cheetah Run, Walker seed 2 in median scenario).\n\n- **The 008c vs 008d comparison still confounds two variables.** While 008f resolves the dt_embedding conditioning confound, the paper still references the 008c vs 008d comparison as supporting evidence. These two experiments differ in both dt_embedding training status AND subsample ON/OFF (hence RSSM training data composition). The paper should clearly relegate this comparison to preliminary/motivating evidence rather than treating it as a controlled experiment.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. Present the full factorial grid explicitly, marking completed vs missing cells.\n2. Replace \"root cause\" language with \"primary contributing factor\" or \"sufficient accelerant\" -- the data support the latter, not the former.\n3. Clarify the logical status of each comparison: which are controlled experiments (single variable changed) vs. which are suggestive but confounded.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Rigor — **5/10**\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The baseline calibration is thorough and provides a solid reference.** 3 seeds with CV<2% on eval return and tight v/e calibration curves (std ~0.014 on v/e ratio). This gives the normalized v/e metric a reliable denominator.\n\n- **The 008d collapse replicates across 2/3 seeds with consistent nve trajectories.** Seeds 0 and 1 show accelerating nve (>2.0x at 45K) with declining eval returns. The pattern is qualitatively consistent even when the third seed shows degradation without full collapse.\n\n- **The 008f stability is robust across all 3 seeds.** nve converges to 0.68-0.82x range with no seed showing collapse. This is the cleanest multi-seed result in the paper.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **3 seeds is below the standard expected at top ML venues for RL experiments.** DreamerV3 on DMC is notoriously high-variance (especially for locomotion tasks with contact dynamics). The RL community standard for claims about \"consistent\" behavior is 5-10 seeds. With 3 seeds, the 008d result (2/3 collapse, 1/3 degradation) has a wide confidence interval -- it's equally consistent with a 40% collapse rate or a 90% collapse rate. The binary \"collapse vs no collapse\" framing relies on n=3 to claim near-certainty.\n\n- **The Cheetah Run results undermine the \"9/9 collapse\" headline without adequate discussion.** The paper summary claims \"2/3 seeds collapse, 1/3 degradation\" in Walker Walk 008d, yet Cheetah Run trained dt_emb shows 0/3 collapse (only degradation with nve 1.25-1.52x, borderline). This means the total collapse count across environments is actually 2/9 (or 3/9 counting Hopper's 1/3), not 9/9. The environment-dependent modulation framing (Section 4.5) is a reasonable response, but the paper needs to lead with this nuance rather than burying it.\n\n- **The Hopper Hop results are too thin.** Section 4.7 reports nve 1.55x and 1/3 collapse but provides no detailed per-seed tables, no learning curves, and no comparison with Hopper-specific baselines. This asymmetry in depth (10 subsections for Walker, 2 sentences for Hopper) raises questions about cherry-picking.\n\n- **Statistical testing is absent.** No confidence intervals, no hypothesis tests, no effect size calculations beyond informal nve comparisons. The Cohen's d claim (\">10\") is stated without the actual computation. For a paper whose central contribution is empirical diagnosis, formal statistical treatment is expected.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. Run 5 seeds minimum for the core comparison (008d vs 008f in Walker Walk).\n2. Report confidence intervals on all key metrics (nve, eval return).\n3. Provide equal depth for all three environments or explicitly scope claims to Walker Walk with Cheetah/Hopper as supporting evidence.\n4. Compute and report actual Cohen's d, p-values from permutation tests or Mann-Whitney U for the 008d vs 008f comparison.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Clarity of Experimental Presentation — **7/10**\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The section structure (4.1-4.10) follows the causal chain logically.** Each subsection addresses a specific question, building from baseline calibration through collapse replication, causal control, environment generalization, and mechanistic analysis. A reader can follow the investigative logic sequentially.\n\n- **The normalized v/e metric is well-defined and consistently applied.** The definition (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) is introduced early (Section 3) and used uniformly throughout. The three-zone interpretation (>1.5x pathological, 0.8-1.2x normal, <0.7x suppression) provides clear anchors.\n\n- **The paper correctly identifies itself as diagnostic rather than algorithmic.** This sets appropriate expectations for the reader.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The experiment naming convention (008a, 008c, 008d, 008e_v3, 008f) is opaque.** These IDs carry no mnemonic information. The paper would benefit from descriptive shorthand (e.g., \"Trained-H8\", \"NoEmb-H8\", \"Untrained-H15\") used alongside or in place of the registry IDs.\n\n- **The relationship between Sections 4.2 and 4.5 creates confusion about the collapse claim.** Section 4.2 establishes collapse in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds), then Section 4.5 reveals Cheetah Run doesn't collapse. A reader who reaches 4.2's conclusion (\"trained dt_embedding causes collapse\") must substantially revise that understanding at 4.5 (\"...but only in Walker Walk\"). The paper should signal this environment-dependence earlier, perhaps in the introduction or at the end of Section 4.2.\n\n- **Tables/figures for Hopper Hop (Section 4.7) are underdeveloped relative to the other environments.** If Hopper is included, it should receive comparable treatment; if it's preliminary, it should be explicitly labeled as such or moved to supplementary material.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. Adopt descriptive configuration names throughout.\n2. Add a single summary table early (Section 3 or beginning of Section 4) showing all configurations with their key parameters and status.\n3. Signal environment-dependent modulation in the introduction so Section 4.5 is expected rather than surprising.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Completeness of Experiments — **5/10**\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The Walker Walk experimental suite is thorough.** Sections 4.1-4.4 cover baseline calibration, collapse replication, causal control (008f), and value suppression (008a) with multi-seed data. The 008d vs 008f comparison directly addresses the central claim.\n\n- **The analysis experiments (Sections 4.6-4.10) add genuine mechanistic depth.** The RSSM fidelity gap analysis, actor entropy analysis, value calibration, and embedding cosine similarity each provide independent evidence for different links in the causal chain. These are not just supplementary figures -- they substantively strengthen the argument.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Missing critical experiments that would strengthen the claims:**\n\n  1. **Trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15**: This would test whether trained dt_embedding causes collapse even without the subsample-induced training data shift. Currently, all trained dt_emb experiments use subsample ON, so there's a residual confound between \"RSSM trained on dt=2 transitions\" and \"dt_embedding trained.\"\n\n  2. **dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15**: The causal control (008f) uses H=8. Would removing dt_embedding at H=15 (matching 008a's horizon) also prevent collapse? This tests whether the H=8/H=15 difference interacts with the dt_embedding effect.\n\n  3. **Frozen trained dt_embedding**: The paper compares trained vs untrained (random frozen) vs removed. A fourth condition -- dt_embedding trained for N steps then frozen -- would test whether the critical factor is the ongoing co-adaptation of dt_embedding and value function, or simply the existence of differentiated embeddings.\n\n  4. **A non-locomotion DMC environment**: All three environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) are locomotion tasks with similar dynamics structures. Testing on a manipulation or balance task would strengthen the generality claim.\n\n- **The Cheetah Run \"degradation not collapse\" result is under-analyzed.** The paper reports this as \"environment-dependent modulation\" (Section 4.5) and links it to RSSM prediction quality (Section 4.6), but doesn't investigate whether the degradation would eventually become collapse given more training steps. Running Cheetah 008d-equivalent to 200K+ steps would determine whether the difference is qualitative (different failure mode) or quantitative (same failure mode, delayed onset).\n\n- **No ablation on dt_embedding dimension.** The paper compares dim=32 vs dim=0. Is there a smooth transition? Does dim=4 or dim=8 produce intermediate overestimation? This would strengthen the mechanistic claim.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. Prioritize the trained dt_emb + subsample OFF experiment -- it directly addresses the remaining confound.\n2. Run Cheetah Run trained dt_emb to 200K+ to determine if degradation eventually becomes collapse.\n3. At minimum, add the dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 cell to the factorial grid.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Soundness of the Diagnostic Metric (nve) — **6/10**\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The normalization by baseline v/e at the same training step is elegant and necessary.** Raw v/e ratios are phase-dependent (baseline v/e goes from 9% to 70% over training), so comparing absolute v/e across time or configurations is meaningless. The normalization removes this confound.\n\n- **The metric correctly identifies pathological cases.** In Walker Walk, all collapsed configurations show nve >1.5x before collapse, and all stable configurations show nve <1.0x. The separation is clean in this environment.\n\n- **The metric is simple, cheap to compute, and interpretable.** This gives it practical value as a monitoring tool during training.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The 1.5x threshold is derived from and evaluated on the same data.** There is no independent validation. The paper reports nve values ranging from 0.55x (stable) to 2.15x (collapse) with a clean gap around 1.0-1.5x. But this gap may be an artifact of the specific configurations tested. A threshold derived post-hoc from the experimental data cannot be presented as a general diagnostic criterion without validation on held-out configurations or environments.\n\n- **The metric's behavior in Cheetah Run challenges its universality.** Cheetah trained dt_emb shows nve 1.25-1.52x -- borderline by the 1.5x threshold -- yet does not collapse. The paper frames this as \"environment-dependent modulation,\" which is reasonable, but it means the 1.5x threshold is not environment-agnostic despite the paper's claim. The metric correctly identifies the direction (elevated nve = concerning) but the threshold doesn't transfer.\n\n- **The metric conflates two distinct phenomena.** High nve can arise from (a) the value function overestimating due to model exploitation, or (b) the evaluation return being depressed by a bad policy while the value function is calibrated to an earlier, better policy. The paper doesn't distinguish these cases. In the early stages of collapse (e.g., 008d at 25K), is nve high because V is too high, or because eval_return is already dropping? Plotting V(s) and eval_return trajectories separately would resolve this ambiguity.\n\n- **No sensitivity analysis.** How much does the nve trajectory change if baseline v/e is computed from a different seed? The baseline v/e std is ~0.014 across seeds, which translates to ~5% nve noise. Is this enough to move a borderline case (nve 1.47x in Cheetah) above or below threshold?\n\n- **The metric is undefined or degenerate when baseline v/e is near zero.** At step 5K, baseline v/e is ~1% (value_mean near zero). Dividing by near-zero produces noise. The paper should specify a minimum step or minimum baseline v/e below which nve is not computed.\n\n### Suggestions\n1. Report the actual gap between the highest stable nve and lowest collapsed nve. If the gap is [0.95, 1.52], any threshold in that range works, and the choice of 1.5x is less critical. If the gap is [1.40, 1.52], the threshold is fragile.\n2. Add sensitivity analysis: how does nve change with +-1 std on the baseline v/e reference?\n3. Discuss the degenerate early-training regime and specify a valid computation window.\n4. Consider supplementing nve with a direct value calibration metric (V(s) vs MC return correlation, as in Section 4.9) for disambiguation.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis paper identifies a genuine and specific failure mechanism -- trained dt-embedding enabling value overestimation via RSSM fidelity gaps -- and the core controlled experiment (008d vs 008f) is methodologically strong. The multi-link causal chain with independent evidence at each step represents careful scientific investigation. The normalized v/e metric, while imperfect, is a useful contribution.\n\nHowever, several issues collectively place the paper below the acceptance threshold in its current form:\n\n1. **The generality claims exceed the evidence.** The paper's strongest results come from a single environment (Walker Walk). Cheetah Run shows degradation rather than collapse, and Hopper Hop is underreported. The \"environment-dependent modulation\" framing is a reasonable interpretation but weakens the diagnostic's claimed universality.\n\n2. **Statistical rigor is insufficient.** 3 seeds is too few for binary collapse/non-collapse claims in RL. No formal statistical tests, no confidence intervals, no rigorous effect size reporting. The 1.5x threshold is post-hoc with no validation.\n\n3. **The factorial design has important missing cells.** The absence of trained dt_emb + subsample OFF and dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 leaves residual confounds that weaken the \"single variable isolation\" narrative.\n\n4. **No engagement with fixability.** A diagnostic paper's value depends partly on whether the diagnosis enables solutions. The paper stops at \"don't do this\" without testing any mitigation.\n\n---\n\n## Top 3 Strengths\n\n**S1. The 008d vs 008f controlled experiment is exemplary.** Single-variable isolation (dt_embed_dim 32 vs 0) with large effect size (nve 1.71x vs 0.73x at 35K) and consistent replication (3/3 stable vs 2/3 collapse). This is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper.\n\n**S2. The multi-link causal chain with independent verification is rigorous.** RSSM fidelity gap -> embedding differentiation -> value overestimation -> actor entropy rise -> collapse. Each link has its own dedicated analysis (Sections 4.6, 4.10, 4.2/4.3, 4.8, 4.9). This structure goes beyond correlation to provide genuine mechanistic understanding.\n\n**S3. The normalized v/e overshoot ratio is a practical diagnostic contribution.** Despite its limitations (post-hoc threshold, environment-dependent calibration), the metric is simple, interpretable, and correctly separates healthy from pathological regimes in the tested configurations. It could see adoption as a monitoring tool in MBRL temporal abstraction research.\n\n---\n\n## Top 3 Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Statistical underpowering and absent formal testing.** 3 seeds per configuration is insufficient for the claims made. The 008d result (2/3 collapse in Walker, 0/3 in Cheetah) has wide confidence intervals that the paper does not report. No hypothesis tests, no confidence intervals, no formally computed effect sizes. For a paper whose contribution is empirical diagnosis, this is a significant gap.\n\n**W2. Environment generalization is weaker than claimed.** The causal mechanism (trained dt_embedding -> delusional critic) produces collapse only in Walker Walk. In Cheetah Run, the same mechanism produces gradual degradation with borderline nve (1.47x mean). In Hopper Hop, only 1/3 seeds collapse. The 1.5x threshold and the \"environment-agnostic diagnostic\" framing are not supported by the cross-environment data.\n\n**W3. Incomplete factorial design leaves residual confounds.** The smoking-gun pair (008d/008f) uses H=8, while the key C3 result (008a) uses H=15 + subsample OFF. Without a trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 cell, the paper cannot rule out that the interaction of subsample ON + trained dt_emb (not trained dt_emb alone) drives collapse. Similarly, the absence of dt_embed_dim=0 + H=15 means we don't know if the dt_embedding effect is horizon-dependent.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The Cheetah Run trained dt_emb configuration shows nve 1.47x at 55K (below the 1.5x threshold) with no collapse. What happens if you run this configuration to 200K steps? Does the degradation eventually become collapse (same mechanism, delayed onset) or does it plateau (qualitatively different)?\n\n**Q2.** What is the actual separation gap between the highest nve of any stable seed and the lowest nve of any collapsed seed across all environments? This would let us evaluate the robustness of ANY threshold, not just 1.5x.\n\n**Q3.** Have you tried training the dt_embedding for a fixed number of steps and then freezing it? This would test whether the ongoing co-adaptation of dt_embedding and value function is critical, or whether having differentiated (but static) embeddings is sufficient to trigger collapse. The distinction matters for mitigation strategies.\n\n**Q4.** The baseline v/e stabilizes at ~70%, meaning DreamerV3 systematically overestimates values even at dt=1. What mechanistically distinguishes this \"tolerable\" overestimation from the \"delusional\" overestimation at dt>1? Is there a qualitative phase transition (e.g., self-reinforcing feedback loop) or merely a matter of degree?\n\n**Q5.** The RSSM prediction quality analysis (Section 4.6) shows Walker dt2/dt1 ratio 1.62 vs Cheetah 1.31. With only two data points, the correlation between RSSM fidelity gap and collapse severity is suggestive but not established. Do you have the Hopper Hop RSSM fidelity ratio? If so, does it fall between Walker and Cheetah as predicted?\n\n**Q6.** Section 4.10 reports cosine similarity dropping from 0.92 to 0.42, co-occurring with nve acceleration. Is this measured from a single seed? What is the cross-seed variance on the cosine similarity trajectory?\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Methodological soundness | 6/10 |\n| Experimental rigor | 5/10 |\n| Clarity of presentation | 7/10 |\n| Completeness of experiments | 5/10 |\n| Soundness of diagnostic metric | 6/10 |\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\n---\n\n## Path to Acceptance\n\nThe core scientific investigation is sound and the central experiment (008d vs 008f) is compelling. To reach acceptance:\n\n1. **Add 2+ seeds** to the core Walker Walk comparisons (008d, 008f) and report formal statistics.\n2. **Fill the critical missing factorial cell**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15.\n3. **Run Cheetah trained dt_emb to 200K+** to determine whether degradation eventually becomes collapse.\n4. **Add threshold sensitivity analysis** and acknowledge that the 1.5x threshold is environment-dependent.\n5. **Scope claims to match evidence**: \"RSSM-based world models\" not \"world-model RL\" generally; \"contributes to collapse in high-RSSM-gap environments\" not \"causes collapse.\"\n6. **Test at least one mitigation** (e.g., conservative value penalty, frozen-after-N-steps dt_embedding) to demonstrate the diagnosis is actionable.\n\nIf the empirical results hold up with more seeds and the claims are properly scoped, this could be a solid 4-5/6 paper.\n","numLines":235,"startLine":1,"totalLines":235}}}
