{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VLh4xTFzcDKgvEvcgLFDoM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T062606\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T07:06:52.845283+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# R2 Review: Novelty/Contribution and Related Work\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\" (best tier)\n11\t\n12\t**Reviewer:** R2 | **Focus:** Novelty/Contribution + Related Work | **Confidence:** 4/5\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Dimension-by-Dimension Evaluation\n17\t\n18\t### 1. Novelty of the Research Question (6/10)\n19\t\n20\t**Strengths:**\n21\t- The question \"why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?\" is well-motivated and genuinely under-explored. Most prior work on temporal abstraction in MBRL has focused on making it work, not on diagnosing why it fails. This failure-analysis framing fills a real gap.\n22\t- The question is practically important: multiple groups have attempted variable-rate imagination in Dreamer-family models, and a systematic diagnosis would save redundant effort.\n23\t\n24\t**Weaknesses:**\n25\t- The question is narrower than the title suggests. It is really \"why does dt-conditioned imagination fail in DreamerV3 specifically,\" not \"why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL\" broadly. The RSSM fidelity gap is an RSSM-specific property. Transformer-based world models (IRIS, TWM, TransDreamer), diffusion world models, or neural ODE world models may exhibit entirely different multi-step prediction degradation profiles. The research question as actually investigated is architecture-specific.\n26\t- That model exploitation in MBRL produces value overestimation is itself well-established (Janner et al. 2019, Chua et al. 2018). The novelty is in the specific mechanism (dt-embedding as the conduit), not the general phenomenon. The paper should be more upfront about this distinction.\n27\t\n28\t**Suggestions:**\n29\t- Retitle to something like \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Duration Conditioning in RSSM-Based World Models\" to match the actual scope of evidence.\n30\t- Add a paragraph explicitly discussing which aspects of the findings might generalize beyond RSSM and which are architecture-specific.\n31\t\n32\t---\n33\t\n34\t### 2. Novelty of Findings (7/10)\n35\t\n36\t**Strengths:**\n37\t- The specific finding that *trained* dt-embedding is the root cause (not RSSM dynamics, not subsampling, not horizon length) is genuinely novel and non-obvious. A naive prediction would be that multi-step RSSM prediction error is the root cause; the insight that the critic's *conditioning on dt* is what enables exploitation of that error is a meaningful contribution.\n38\t- The temporal coupling between embedding differentiation (cosine similarity dropping below ~0.4) and collapse onset provides compelling mechanistic evidence beyond simple correlation.\n39\t- The asymmetry between trained vs. untrained embedding outcomes (value suppression vs. acceleration) is an interesting finding with implications for auxiliary conditioning in MBRL more broadly.\n40\t- The right-skewed overestimation distribution (3.2x at 95th percentile vs. 1.65x at mean) is a useful characterization showing the problem is state-dependent, concentrated in regions of maximal RSSM error.\n41\t\n42\t**Weaknesses:**\n43\t- The normalized v/e overshoot ratio, while useful, is an incremental diagnostic rather than a conceptual breakthrough. The idea of normalizing value overestimation by a baseline's own overestimation trajectory is sensible engineering, but the 1.5x threshold is post-hoc and derived from the same data it is evaluated on (see W4 below).\n44\t- The finding that longer dt amplifies the problem (C4) is unsurprising given that RSSM prediction error compounds with temporal gap.\n45\t- The value growth suppression finding (C3) is interesting but somewhat expected: if the critic cannot distinguish dt values, it cannot form dt-specific overestimates.\n46\t\n47\t**Suggestions:**\n48\t- Strengthen the novelty framing by connecting to the broader principle: auxiliary conditioning variables in MBRL critics can create exploitation channels when the world model's predictions under those conditions are insufficiently accurate. This generalizes beyond dt-embedding.\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t### 3. Significance of Contribution (5/10)\n53\t\n54\t**Strengths:**\n55\t- The concrete negative recommendation (\"don't jointly train dt-embeddings with the value function under current RSSM architectures\") is immediately actionable and could prevent wasted compute in future temporal abstraction attempts.\n56\t- The diagnostic methodology (factorial ablation with controlled single-variable comparisons) is exemplary and could serve as a template for other mechanistic investigations in RL.\n57\t- The paper demonstrates that careful negative results, when well-diagnosed, can be more informative than positive results that work for unclear reasons.\n58\t\n59\t**Weaknesses:**\n60\t- **[CRITICAL] The contribution reduces to a narrow negative finding.** The paper establishes: \"if you add a trained dt-embedding to DreamerV3's critic, it causes value overestimation and collapse.\" The actionable takeaway is: don't do this. This is a valid finding, but its significance is limited by (a) the narrow architectural scope (DreamerV3/RSSM only), (b) the absence of any proposed fix, and (c) the fact that variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 is not itself an established technique that the community is actively pursuing. The paper diagnoses a failure in the authors' own modification, not in a widely-deployed method.\n61\t- **No mitigation pilot.** Section 5.4 lists four mitigation directions in a single paragraph without any experimental validation. For a diagnostic paper to have high impact, it should demonstrate that the diagnosis enables a fix. Even a single pilot experiment (e.g., conservative value regularization, ensemble-based penalties, or the suggested decoupled training schedule) would transform the contribution from \"here's why this fails\" to \"here's why this fails and here's how to fix it.\"\n62\t- **Limited environment diversity.** All three environments are DMC locomotion tasks with continuous control, similar state/action spaces, and dense rewards. The mechanism might behave differently in sparse-reward, high-dimensional, or discrete-action settings. The \"environment-agnostic\" claim for the 1.5x threshold is premature with only locomotion tasks.\n63\t\n64\t**Suggestions:**\n65\t- Add at least one pilot mitigation experiment. The simplest candidate: clip the critic's value predictions when R_norm exceeds a threshold during imagination. Even if imperfect, this would show the diagnosis is actionable.\n66\t- Test on at least one non-locomotion DMC task (e.g., Cartpole Swingup, Reacher Hard, or a manipulation task) to support the \"environment-agnostic\" claim.\n67\t\n68\t---\n69\t\n70\t### 4. Related Work Coverage (3/10)\n71\t\n72\t**Strengths:**\n73\t- The three categories covered (temporal abstraction, world-model RL, value overestimation) are the right ones.\n74\t- The positioning as \"diagnostic, not method\" is clear and appropriate.\n75\t\n76\t**Weaknesses:**\n77\t- **[CRITICAL] The related work is severely underdeveloped for a paper whose contribution is understanding a failure mechanism.** A diagnostic paper must demonstrate deep awareness of what is already known to credibly claim novel insight. With ~10 references, the paper fails to engage with large bodies of directly relevant work.\n78\t\n79\t**Specific gaps:**\n80\t\n81\t**(a) Value overestimation in RL (most critical gap):**\n82\t- **TD3** (Fujimoto et al., 2018): Established that function approximation causes systematic overestimation in actor-critic methods, proposed clipped double-Q. The paper's \"delusional critic\" is an instance of this broader phenomenon, conditioned on dt.\n83\t- **SAC** (Haarnoja et al., 2018): Uses entropy regularization to mitigate overestimation; directly relevant to the actor entropy analysis in Section 4.8.\n84\t- **REDQ** (Chen et al., 2021): Ensemble-based overestimation control, directly applicable as a potential mitigation.\n85\t- **Maxmin Q-learning** (Lan et al., 2020): Another ensemble approach to overestimation.\n86\t- The paper's claim of \"delusional critic\" must be situated against this rich literature. Is the dt-conditioned overestimation qualitatively different from standard double-Q-style overestimation, or is it the same phenomenon amplified by a new conditioning variable?\n87\t\n88\t**(b) Multi-step prediction and value estimation:**\n89\t- **MVE** (Feinberg et al., 2018): Model-based value expansion explicitly studies how model prediction horizon affects value accuracy — the exact phenomenon at the heart of this paper.\n90\t- **STEVE** (Buckman et al., 2018): Stochastic ensemble value expansion, addressing the same model-error-in-value-estimation problem.\n91\t- **H-step model rollouts and compound error** (Talvitie 2014, 2017): The foundational analysis of how model prediction errors compound over multi-step rollouts.\n92\t- These are not tangentially related — they study the same core phenomenon (model prediction quality degrading over temporal horizons and its impact on value estimation) that this paper claims to diagnose.\n93\t\n94\t**(c) Conservative / pessimistic MBRL:**\n95\t- **MOPO** (Yu et al., 2020): Explicitly penalizes value estimates in states with high model uncertainty — directly addresses the \"don't trust value estimates where the model is inaccurate\" principle that this paper discovers empirically.\n96\t- **COMBO** (Yu et al., 2021): Conservative offline model-based RL.\n97\t- **MOReL** (Kidambi et al., 2020): Pessimistic MDP construction for MBRL.\n98\t- The paper's finding that trained dt-embedding enables exploitation of model inaccuracy is a rediscovery, in a specific context, of the principle that motivates this entire line of work. The paper must engage with it.\n99\t\n100\t**(d) Temporal abstraction beyond options/FiGAR:**\n101\t- **Option-Critic** (Bacon et al., 2017): Already in the bibliography but not discussed in the related work section.\n102\t- **THICK** (Gumbsch et al., ICLR 2024): Temporal hierarchy in Dreamer — the most directly comparable work on temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family. Its absence is a significant oversight.\n103\t- **TempoRL** (Biedenkapp et al., 2021): Learned action persistence.\n104\t- **Director** (Hafner et al., 2022): Temporal abstraction in DreamerV2 via goal-conditioned hierarchical control. This is temporal abstraction *within the Dreamer architecture family* and its omission is inexplicable.\n105\t- **TAWM** (Nhu, Son & Lin, ICML 2025): Conditions latent dynamics on temporal gap dt — extremely close to the paper's setup.\n106\t- **LS-Imagine** (Li et al., ICLR 2025): Goal-conditioned jumpy state transitions in world models.\n107\t\n108\t**(e) Delusional policies — misattribution:**\n109\t- The paper cites \"Delalleau et al. (2019)\" for delusional policies. The foundational work is **Lu et al. (2018), \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration,\" NeurIPS 2018**. This is a significant misattribution for a concept that the paper places at its conceptual center.\n110\t\n111\t**(f) Diagnostic/analysis papers in RL:**\n112\t- **Henderson et al. (2018)**: \"Deep RL that Matters\" — methodological template for careful RL analysis.\n113\t- **Engstrom et al. (2020)**: \"Implementation Matters in Deep Policy Gradients\" — another diagnostic RL paper.\n114\t- **Agarwal et al. (2021)**: \"Deep RL at the Edge of the Statistical Precipice\" — statistical methodology for RL evaluation.\n115\t- Positioning against these would strengthen the methodological contribution.\n116\t\n117\t**Suggestions:**\n118\t- Expand related work to at least 25-30 references. The current ~10 references are inadequate for any venue, but especially for a diagnostic paper.\n119\t- Add dedicated paragraphs on (i) value overestimation in actor-critic methods, (ii) multi-step value estimation and compound model error, (iii) conservative/pessimistic MBRL, and (iv) recent temporal abstraction in world models.\n120\t- Correct the delusional policies citation to Lu et al. (2018).\n121\t- Discuss whether existing mitigations (TD3 clipped double-Q, MOPO uncertainty penalties, ensemble methods) could address the specific failure mode identified here.\n122\t\n123\t---\n124\t\n125\t### 5. Framing and Positioning (5/10)\n126\t\n127\t**Strengths:**\n128\t- The paper does not overclaim a new method — the diagnostic framing is honest and appropriate.\n129\t- The causal chain narrative (fidelity gap -> embedding differentiation -> delusional critic -> actor degradation -> collapse) is well-structured and each link is independently verified.\n130\t- The paper correctly identifies its own boundary conditions (RSSM-specific, locomotion tasks, architecture-specific).\n131\t\n132\t**Weaknesses:**\n133\t- **Title overclaims.** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning\" implies generality across all world-model architectures and all forms of temporal abstraction. The paper studies one specific form (dt-conditioned imagination) in one specific architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM). The title should reflect this.\n134\t- **The \"delusional critic\" terminology risks confusion.** The term \"delusional\" in RL has a specific formal meaning (Lu et al., 2018) relating to value function approximation producing internally consistent but unrealizable policies. The paper's usage is related but not identical — the overestimation here arises from model inaccuracy combined with conditioning, not from function approximation class constraints. This terminological borrowing should be made precise.\n135\t- **The paper undersells what is actually novel.** The most interesting insight — that auxiliary conditioning variables in critics can create exploitation channels proportional to model inaccuracy under those conditions — is a general principle buried in a specific case study. The paper would benefit from explicitly stating this general principle and discussing its implications beyond dt-embedding.\n136\t- **The \"environment-agnostic\" claim for the 1.5x threshold is overstated.** Three DMC locomotion tasks with similar dynamics and reward structures do not establish environment-agnosticism. The threshold was also derived from the same data it is evaluated on.\n137\t\n138\t**Suggestions:**\n139\t- Scope the title: \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Duration Conditioning in RSSM-Based World Models.\"\n140\t- Explicitly state the general principle (auxiliary conditioning + model inaccuracy = exploitation channel) and discuss when it applies beyond dt-embedding.\n141\t- Replace \"environment-agnostic\" with \"consistent across tested environments\" or similar.\n142\t\n143\t---\n144\t\n145\t## Overall Assessment\n146\t\n147\t### Top 3 Strengths\n148\t\n149\t1. **Methodologically exemplary core experiment (008d vs 008f).** Single-variable isolation with binary outcome (3/3 collapse vs 0/3 stable) and massive effect size. This is how controlled experiments in RL should be designed.\n150\t\n151\t2. **Non-obvious mechanistic insight.** The finding that the *critic's conditioning on dt* (rather than the RSSM's prediction quality at dt>1 per se) is the root cause of collapse is genuinely surprising and useful. The temporal coupling with embedding differentiation provides causal pathway evidence.\n152\t\n153\t3. **Honest diagnostic framing.** The paper does not overclaim a new method or state-of-the-art results. It asks \"why does this fail?\" and provides a rigorous answer. This genre of paper is valuable and underrepresented in the field.\n154\t\n155\t### Top 3 Weaknesses\n156\t\n157\t1. **[CRITICAL] Data provenance concern.** Cross-referencing the paper's claims against the project memory and experiment registry reveals that only single-seed Walker Walk experiments appear to have been completed. The \"9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable\" headline — the paper's central quantitative claim — appears substantially built on projected/simulated data (multi-seed Walker Walk seeds 1-2, all Cheetah Run, all Hopper Hop). The paper does not distinguish projected from empirical results. If confirmed, this is a fatal flaw. The paper must either complete these experiments or clearly label projections.\n158\t\n159\t2. **[CRITICAL] Related work is grossly inadequate.** ~10 references for a diagnostic paper that claims to explain a failure mechanism in MBRL. Missing entire bodies of directly relevant work: value overestimation literature (TD3, SAC, REDQ), multi-step value estimation (MVE, STEVE), conservative MBRL (MOPO, COMBO), recent temporal abstraction in world models (THICK, Director, TAWM, LS-Imagine), and the correct citation for delusional policies (Lu et al. 2018, not Delalleau et al. 2019). A diagnostic paper must demonstrate mastery of prior knowledge to credibly claim novelty.\n160\t\n161\t3. **Narrow scope presented as general.** The title claims to explain why temporal abstraction fails in world-model RL generally, but all evidence comes from one architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM) on one task family (DMC locomotion). The RSSM fidelity gap is architecture-specific. The \"environment-agnostic\" diagnostic threshold claim rests on 3 similar environments. No mitigation is tested. The contribution is real but narrower than framed.\n162\t\n163\t### Missing References (must-cite)\n164\t\n165\t1. **Lu et al. (2018)** — \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration,\" NeurIPS. The actual foundational work on delusional policies. Currently misattributed.\n166\t2. **Fujimoto et al. (2018)** — TD3. Systematic overestimation in actor-critic methods.\n167\t3. **Feinberg et al. (2018)** — MVE. Model-based value expansion; directly studies model horizon vs value accuracy.\n168\t4. **Buckman et al. (2018)** — STEVE. Stochastic ensemble value expansion.\n169\t5. **Yu et al. (2020)** — MOPO. Model-based offline policy optimization with uncertainty penalties. Directly addresses \"don't trust values where model is inaccurate.\"\n170\t6. **Gumbsch et al. (2024)** — THICK. Temporal hierarchy in Dreamer; most directly comparable work.\n171\t7. **Hafner et al. (2022)** — Director. Temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family.\n172\t8. **Biedenkapp et al. (2021)** — TempoRL. Learned action persistence.\n173\t9. **Talvitie (2014, 2017)** — Compound error in multi-step model predictions.\n174\t10. **Chen et al. (2021)** — REDQ. Ensemble-based overestimation control.\n175\t11. **Kumar et al. (2020)** — CQL. Conservative Q-learning; relevant to mitigation discussion.\n176\t12. **Henderson et al. (2018)** — \"Deep RL that Matters.\" Diagnostic RL methodology.\n177\t13. **Agarwal et al. (2021)** — Statistical evaluation methodology for RL.\n178\t\n179\t### Questions for Authors\n180\t\n181\t**Q1.** Which of the reported multi-seed and multi-environment results are from completed experiments? Specifically: are Walker Walk seeds 1-2 (for 008d, 008f, 008a), all Cheetah Run results, and all Hopper Hop results from actual training runs or from projections?\n182\t\n183\t**Q2.** The paper's central concept is \"delusional critic,\" citing Delalleau et al. (2019). The foundational work on delusional policies is Lu et al. (2018, NeurIPS). Can you clarify this citation and explain how your usage of \"delusional\" relates to the formal definition in Lu et al.?\n184\t\n185\t**Q3.** How does the dt-conditioned overestimation relate to the standard overestimation studied in TD3? Is it qualitatively different (a new failure mode) or quantitatively different (the same phenomenon amplified by dt-conditioning)? Could clipped double-Q or ensemble critics mitigate it?\n186\t\n187\t**Q4.** MOPO (Yu et al., 2020) explicitly penalizes value estimates proportional to model uncertainty. Your finding is essentially that the critic exploits model inaccuracy at dt>1. Have you considered whether MOPO-style uncertainty penalties would directly address this — and if so, why is this not tested as a mitigation pilot?\n188\t\n189\t**Q5.** THICK (Gumbsch et al., ICLR 2024) implements temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family via learned temporal hierarchy. Do they observe similar value overestimation? If not, what architectural difference prevents it? This comparison seems essential for a paper claiming to diagnose temporal abstraction failure in world-model RL.\n190\t\n191\t**Q6.** The general principle emerging from your findings seems to be: \"auxiliary conditioning variables in critics create exploitation channels when the world model is inaccurate under those conditions.\" Do you agree this is the general takeaway, and if so, does it apply to other conditioning variables beyond dt (e.g., goal conditioning, skill conditioning in hierarchical RL)?\n192\t\n193\t**Q7.** The 1.5x threshold perfectly separates all 18 data points. What is the actual gap between the highest R_norm of any stable run and the lowest R_norm of any collapsed run? Is there a continuous phase transition or a sharp boundary?\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## Scores\n198\t\n199\t| Dimension | Score |\n200\t|-----------|-------|\n201\t| Novelty of research question | 6/10 |\n202\t| Novelty of findings | 7/10 |\n203\t| Significance of contribution | 5/10 |\n204\t| Related work coverage | 3/10 |\n205\t| Framing and positioning | 5/10 |\n206\t\n207\t**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n208\t\n209\t**Confidence: 4/5**\n210\t\n211\t---\n212\t\n213\t## Path to Acceptance\n214\t\n215\tThe core scientific insight (trained dt-embedding as exploitation channel) is genuine and the 008d vs 008f experiment is well-designed. To reach acceptance:\n216\t\n217\t1. **Complete all claimed experiments** or clearly label projections. The 9/9 headline cannot stand on projected data.\n218\t2. **Expand related work to 25-30 references** covering value overestimation (TD3, REDQ), multi-step value estimation (MVE, STEVE), conservative MBRL (MOPO), recent temporal abstraction in world models (THICK, Director, TAWM), and correct the delusional policies citation.\n219\t3. **Scope the title and claims** to RSSM-based models and tested environments.\n220\t4. **Add one mitigation pilot** (e.g., MOPO-style uncertainty penalty, ensemble critics, or conservative value clipping) to show the diagnosis is actionable.\n221\t5. **Add threshold sensitivity analysis** — report the actual separation gap and discuss whether 1.5x is a phase transition or a post-hoc cutoff.\n222\t\n223\tIf the empirical results match projections and these revisions are made, the paper could reach 4-5/6.\n224\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a90c8859-8e50-4bd7-a75e-c4f31c8dbc1b","uuid":"17814383-4e1b-411a-a85b-797372c3eef4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T07:08:58.575Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T062606/best/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T062606\ntier: best\noverall_score: weak reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T07:06:52.845283+00:00\n---\n\n# R2 Review: Novelty/Contribution and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation in Temporally-Abstract World Models\" (best tier)\n\n**Reviewer:** R2 | **Focus:** Novelty/Contribution + Related Work | **Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Dimension-by-Dimension Evaluation\n\n### 1. Novelty of the Research Question (6/10)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The question \"why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?\" is well-motivated and genuinely under-explored. Most prior work on temporal abstraction in MBRL has focused on making it work, not on diagnosing why it fails. This failure-analysis framing fills a real gap.\n- The question is practically important: multiple groups have attempted variable-rate imagination in Dreamer-family models, and a systematic diagnosis would save redundant effort.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- The question is narrower than the title suggests. It is really \"why does dt-conditioned imagination fail in DreamerV3 specifically,\" not \"why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL\" broadly. The RSSM fidelity gap is an RSSM-specific property. Transformer-based world models (IRIS, TWM, TransDreamer), diffusion world models, or neural ODE world models may exhibit entirely different multi-step prediction degradation profiles. The research question as actually investigated is architecture-specific.\n- That model exploitation in MBRL produces value overestimation is itself well-established (Janner et al. 2019, Chua et al. 2018). The novelty is in the specific mechanism (dt-embedding as the conduit), not the general phenomenon. The paper should be more upfront about this distinction.\n\n**Suggestions:**\n- Retitle to something like \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Duration Conditioning in RSSM-Based World Models\" to match the actual scope of evidence.\n- Add a paragraph explicitly discussing which aspects of the findings might generalize beyond RSSM and which are architecture-specific.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Novelty of Findings (7/10)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The specific finding that *trained* dt-embedding is the root cause (not RSSM dynamics, not subsampling, not horizon length) is genuinely novel and non-obvious. A naive prediction would be that multi-step RSSM prediction error is the root cause; the insight that the critic's *conditioning on dt* is what enables exploitation of that error is a meaningful contribution.\n- The temporal coupling between embedding differentiation (cosine similarity dropping below ~0.4) and collapse onset provides compelling mechanistic evidence beyond simple correlation.\n- The asymmetry between trained vs. untrained embedding outcomes (value suppression vs. acceleration) is an interesting finding with implications for auxiliary conditioning in MBRL more broadly.\n- The right-skewed overestimation distribution (3.2x at 95th percentile vs. 1.65x at mean) is a useful characterization showing the problem is state-dependent, concentrated in regions of maximal RSSM error.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- The normalized v/e overshoot ratio, while useful, is an incremental diagnostic rather than a conceptual breakthrough. The idea of normalizing value overestimation by a baseline's own overestimation trajectory is sensible engineering, but the 1.5x threshold is post-hoc and derived from the same data it is evaluated on (see W4 below).\n- The finding that longer dt amplifies the problem (C4) is unsurprising given that RSSM prediction error compounds with temporal gap.\n- The value growth suppression finding (C3) is interesting but somewhat expected: if the critic cannot distinguish dt values, it cannot form dt-specific overestimates.\n\n**Suggestions:**\n- Strengthen the novelty framing by connecting to the broader principle: auxiliary conditioning variables in MBRL critics can create exploitation channels when the world model's predictions under those conditions are insufficiently accurate. This generalizes beyond dt-embedding.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Significance of Contribution (5/10)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The concrete negative recommendation (\"don't jointly train dt-embeddings with the value function under current RSSM architectures\") is immediately actionable and could prevent wasted compute in future temporal abstraction attempts.\n- The diagnostic methodology (factorial ablation with controlled single-variable comparisons) is exemplary and could serve as a template for other mechanistic investigations in RL.\n- The paper demonstrates that careful negative results, when well-diagnosed, can be more informative than positive results that work for unclear reasons.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **[CRITICAL] The contribution reduces to a narrow negative finding.** The paper establishes: \"if you add a trained dt-embedding to DreamerV3's critic, it causes value overestimation and collapse.\" The actionable takeaway is: don't do this. This is a valid finding, but its significance is limited by (a) the narrow architectural scope (DreamerV3/RSSM only), (b) the absence of any proposed fix, and (c) the fact that variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 is not itself an established technique that the community is actively pursuing. The paper diagnoses a failure in the authors' own modification, not in a widely-deployed method.\n- **No mitigation pilot.** Section 5.4 lists four mitigation directions in a single paragraph without any experimental validation. For a diagnostic paper to have high impact, it should demonstrate that the diagnosis enables a fix. Even a single pilot experiment (e.g., conservative value regularization, ensemble-based penalties, or the suggested decoupled training schedule) would transform the contribution from \"here's why this fails\" to \"here's why this fails and here's how to fix it.\"\n- **Limited environment diversity.** All three environments are DMC locomotion tasks with continuous control, similar state/action spaces, and dense rewards. The mechanism might behave differently in sparse-reward, high-dimensional, or discrete-action settings. The \"environment-agnostic\" claim for the 1.5x threshold is premature with only locomotion tasks.\n\n**Suggestions:**\n- Add at least one pilot mitigation experiment. The simplest candidate: clip the critic's value predictions when R_norm exceeds a threshold during imagination. Even if imperfect, this would show the diagnosis is actionable.\n- Test on at least one non-locomotion DMC task (e.g., Cartpole Swingup, Reacher Hard, or a manipulation task) to support the \"environment-agnostic\" claim.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Related Work Coverage (3/10)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The three categories covered (temporal abstraction, world-model RL, value overestimation) are the right ones.\n- The positioning as \"diagnostic, not method\" is clear and appropriate.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **[CRITICAL] The related work is severely underdeveloped for a paper whose contribution is understanding a failure mechanism.** A diagnostic paper must demonstrate deep awareness of what is already known to credibly claim novel insight. With ~10 references, the paper fails to engage with large bodies of directly relevant work.\n\n**Specific gaps:**\n\n**(a) Value overestimation in RL (most critical gap):**\n- **TD3** (Fujimoto et al., 2018): Established that function approximation causes systematic overestimation in actor-critic methods, proposed clipped double-Q. The paper's \"delusional critic\" is an instance of this broader phenomenon, conditioned on dt.\n- **SAC** (Haarnoja et al., 2018): Uses entropy regularization to mitigate overestimation; directly relevant to the actor entropy analysis in Section 4.8.\n- **REDQ** (Chen et al., 2021): Ensemble-based overestimation control, directly applicable as a potential mitigation.\n- **Maxmin Q-learning** (Lan et al., 2020): Another ensemble approach to overestimation.\n- The paper's claim of \"delusional critic\" must be situated against this rich literature. Is the dt-conditioned overestimation qualitatively different from standard double-Q-style overestimation, or is it the same phenomenon amplified by a new conditioning variable?\n\n**(b) Multi-step prediction and value estimation:**\n- **MVE** (Feinberg et al., 2018): Model-based value expansion explicitly studies how model prediction horizon affects value accuracy — the exact phenomenon at the heart of this paper.\n- **STEVE** (Buckman et al., 2018): Stochastic ensemble value expansion, addressing the same model-error-in-value-estimation problem.\n- **H-step model rollouts and compound error** (Talvitie 2014, 2017): The foundational analysis of how model prediction errors compound over multi-step rollouts.\n- These are not tangentially related — they study the same core phenomenon (model prediction quality degrading over temporal horizons and its impact on value estimation) that this paper claims to diagnose.\n\n**(c) Conservative / pessimistic MBRL:**\n- **MOPO** (Yu et al., 2020): Explicitly penalizes value estimates in states with high model uncertainty — directly addresses the \"don't trust value estimates where the model is inaccurate\" principle that this paper discovers empirically.\n- **COMBO** (Yu et al., 2021): Conservative offline model-based RL.\n- **MOReL** (Kidambi et al., 2020): Pessimistic MDP construction for MBRL.\n- The paper's finding that trained dt-embedding enables exploitation of model inaccuracy is a rediscovery, in a specific context, of the principle that motivates this entire line of work. The paper must engage with it.\n\n**(d) Temporal abstraction beyond options/FiGAR:**\n- **Option-Critic** (Bacon et al., 2017): Already in the bibliography but not discussed in the related work section.\n- **THICK** (Gumbsch et al., ICLR 2024): Temporal hierarchy in Dreamer — the most directly comparable work on temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family. Its absence is a significant oversight.\n- **TempoRL** (Biedenkapp et al., 2021): Learned action persistence.\n- **Director** (Hafner et al., 2022): Temporal abstraction in DreamerV2 via goal-conditioned hierarchical control. This is temporal abstraction *within the Dreamer architecture family* and its omission is inexplicable.\n- **TAWM** (Nhu, Son & Lin, ICML 2025): Conditions latent dynamics on temporal gap dt — extremely close to the paper's setup.\n- **LS-Imagine** (Li et al., ICLR 2025): Goal-conditioned jumpy state transitions in world models.\n\n**(e) Delusional policies — misattribution:**\n- The paper cites \"Delalleau et al. (2019)\" for delusional policies. The foundational work is **Lu et al. (2018), \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration,\" NeurIPS 2018**. This is a significant misattribution for a concept that the paper places at its conceptual center.\n\n**(f) Diagnostic/analysis papers in RL:**\n- **Henderson et al. (2018)**: \"Deep RL that Matters\" — methodological template for careful RL analysis.\n- **Engstrom et al. (2020)**: \"Implementation Matters in Deep Policy Gradients\" — another diagnostic RL paper.\n- **Agarwal et al. (2021)**: \"Deep RL at the Edge of the Statistical Precipice\" — statistical methodology for RL evaluation.\n- Positioning against these would strengthen the methodological contribution.\n\n**Suggestions:**\n- Expand related work to at least 25-30 references. The current ~10 references are inadequate for any venue, but especially for a diagnostic paper.\n- Add dedicated paragraphs on (i) value overestimation in actor-critic methods, (ii) multi-step value estimation and compound model error, (iii) conservative/pessimistic MBRL, and (iv) recent temporal abstraction in world models.\n- Correct the delusional policies citation to Lu et al. (2018).\n- Discuss whether existing mitigations (TD3 clipped double-Q, MOPO uncertainty penalties, ensemble methods) could address the specific failure mode identified here.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Framing and Positioning (5/10)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The paper does not overclaim a new method — the diagnostic framing is honest and appropriate.\n- The causal chain narrative (fidelity gap -> embedding differentiation -> delusional critic -> actor degradation -> collapse) is well-structured and each link is independently verified.\n- The paper correctly identifies its own boundary conditions (RSSM-specific, locomotion tasks, architecture-specific).\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **Title overclaims.** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning\" implies generality across all world-model architectures and all forms of temporal abstraction. The paper studies one specific form (dt-conditioned imagination) in one specific architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM). The title should reflect this.\n- **The \"delusional critic\" terminology risks confusion.** The term \"delusional\" in RL has a specific formal meaning (Lu et al., 2018) relating to value function approximation producing internally consistent but unrealizable policies. The paper's usage is related but not identical — the overestimation here arises from model inaccuracy combined with conditioning, not from function approximation class constraints. This terminological borrowing should be made precise.\n- **The paper undersells what is actually novel.** The most interesting insight — that auxiliary conditioning variables in critics can create exploitation channels proportional to model inaccuracy under those conditions — is a general principle buried in a specific case study. The paper would benefit from explicitly stating this general principle and discussing its implications beyond dt-embedding.\n- **The \"environment-agnostic\" claim for the 1.5x threshold is overstated.** Three DMC locomotion tasks with similar dynamics and reward structures do not establish environment-agnosticism. The threshold was also derived from the same data it is evaluated on.\n\n**Suggestions:**\n- Scope the title: \"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Duration Conditioning in RSSM-Based World Models.\"\n- Explicitly state the general principle (auxiliary conditioning + model inaccuracy = exploitation channel) and discuss when it applies beyond dt-embedding.\n- Replace \"environment-agnostic\" with \"consistent across tested environments\" or similar.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n### Top 3 Strengths\n\n1. **Methodologically exemplary core experiment (008d vs 008f).** Single-variable isolation with binary outcome (3/3 collapse vs 0/3 stable) and massive effect size. This is how controlled experiments in RL should be designed.\n\n2. **Non-obvious mechanistic insight.** The finding that the *critic's conditioning on dt* (rather than the RSSM's prediction quality at dt>1 per se) is the root cause of collapse is genuinely surprising and useful. The temporal coupling with embedding differentiation provides causal pathway evidence.\n\n3. **Honest diagnostic framing.** The paper does not overclaim a new method or state-of-the-art results. It asks \"why does this fail?\" and provides a rigorous answer. This genre of paper is valuable and underrepresented in the field.\n\n### Top 3 Weaknesses\n\n1. **[CRITICAL] Data provenance concern.** Cross-referencing the paper's claims against the project memory and experiment registry reveals that only single-seed Walker Walk experiments appear to have been completed. The \"9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable\" headline — the paper's central quantitative claim — appears substantially built on projected/simulated data (multi-seed Walker Walk seeds 1-2, all Cheetah Run, all Hopper Hop). The paper does not distinguish projected from empirical results. If confirmed, this is a fatal flaw. The paper must either complete these experiments or clearly label projections.\n\n2. **[CRITICAL] Related work is grossly inadequate.** ~10 references for a diagnostic paper that claims to explain a failure mechanism in MBRL. Missing entire bodies of directly relevant work: value overestimation literature (TD3, SAC, REDQ), multi-step value estimation (MVE, STEVE), conservative MBRL (MOPO, COMBO), recent temporal abstraction in world models (THICK, Director, TAWM, LS-Imagine), and the correct citation for delusional policies (Lu et al. 2018, not Delalleau et al. 2019). A diagnostic paper must demonstrate mastery of prior knowledge to credibly claim novelty.\n\n3. **Narrow scope presented as general.** The title claims to explain why temporal abstraction fails in world-model RL generally, but all evidence comes from one architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM) on one task family (DMC locomotion). The RSSM fidelity gap is architecture-specific. The \"environment-agnostic\" diagnostic threshold claim rests on 3 similar environments. No mitigation is tested. The contribution is real but narrower than framed.\n\n### Missing References (must-cite)\n\n1. **Lu et al. (2018)** — \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration,\" NeurIPS. The actual foundational work on delusional policies. Currently misattributed.\n2. **Fujimoto et al. (2018)** — TD3. Systematic overestimation in actor-critic methods.\n3. **Feinberg et al. (2018)** — MVE. Model-based value expansion; directly studies model horizon vs value accuracy.\n4. **Buckman et al. (2018)** — STEVE. Stochastic ensemble value expansion.\n5. **Yu et al. (2020)** — MOPO. Model-based offline policy optimization with uncertainty penalties. Directly addresses \"don't trust values where model is inaccurate.\"\n6. **Gumbsch et al. (2024)** — THICK. Temporal hierarchy in Dreamer; most directly comparable work.\n7. **Hafner et al. (2022)** — Director. Temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family.\n8. **Biedenkapp et al. (2021)** — TempoRL. Learned action persistence.\n9. **Talvitie (2014, 2017)** — Compound error in multi-step model predictions.\n10. **Chen et al. (2021)** — REDQ. Ensemble-based overestimation control.\n11. **Kumar et al. (2020)** — CQL. Conservative Q-learning; relevant to mitigation discussion.\n12. **Henderson et al. (2018)** — \"Deep RL that Matters.\" Diagnostic RL methodology.\n13. **Agarwal et al. (2021)** — Statistical evaluation methodology for RL.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Which of the reported multi-seed and multi-environment results are from completed experiments? Specifically: are Walker Walk seeds 1-2 (for 008d, 008f, 008a), all Cheetah Run results, and all Hopper Hop results from actual training runs or from projections?\n\n**Q2.** The paper's central concept is \"delusional critic,\" citing Delalleau et al. (2019). The foundational work on delusional policies is Lu et al. (2018, NeurIPS). Can you clarify this citation and explain how your usage of \"delusional\" relates to the formal definition in Lu et al.?\n\n**Q3.** How does the dt-conditioned overestimation relate to the standard overestimation studied in TD3? Is it qualitatively different (a new failure mode) or quantitatively different (the same phenomenon amplified by dt-conditioning)? Could clipped double-Q or ensemble critics mitigate it?\n\n**Q4.** MOPO (Yu et al., 2020) explicitly penalizes value estimates proportional to model uncertainty. Your finding is essentially that the critic exploits model inaccuracy at dt>1. Have you considered whether MOPO-style uncertainty penalties would directly address this — and if so, why is this not tested as a mitigation pilot?\n\n**Q5.** THICK (Gumbsch et al., ICLR 2024) implements temporal abstraction in the Dreamer family via learned temporal hierarchy. Do they observe similar value overestimation? If not, what architectural difference prevents it? This comparison seems essential for a paper claiming to diagnose temporal abstraction failure in world-model RL.\n\n**Q6.** The general principle emerging from your findings seems to be: \"auxiliary conditioning variables in critics create exploitation channels when the world model is inaccurate under those conditions.\" Do you agree this is the general takeaway, and if so, does it apply to other conditioning variables beyond dt (e.g., goal conditioning, skill conditioning in hierarchical RL)?\n\n**Q7.** The 1.5x threshold perfectly separates all 18 data points. What is the actual gap between the highest R_norm of any stable run and the lowest R_norm of any collapsed run? Is there a continuous phase transition or a sharp boundary?\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Novelty of research question | 6/10 |\n| Novelty of findings | 7/10 |\n| Significance of contribution | 5/10 |\n| Related work coverage | 3/10 |\n| Framing and positioning | 5/10 |\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n---\n\n## Path to Acceptance\n\nThe core scientific insight (trained dt-embedding as exploitation channel) is genuine and the 008d vs 008f experiment is well-designed. To reach acceptance:\n\n1. **Complete all claimed experiments** or clearly label projections. The 9/9 headline cannot stand on projected data.\n2. **Expand related work to 25-30 references** covering value overestimation (TD3, REDQ), multi-step value estimation (MVE, STEVE), conservative MBRL (MOPO), recent temporal abstraction in world models (THICK, Director, TAWM), and correct the delusional policies citation.\n3. **Scope the title and claims** to RSSM-based models and tested environments.\n4. **Add one mitigation pilot** (e.g., MOPO-style uncertainty penalty, ensemble critics, or conservative value clipping) to show the diagnosis is actionable.\n5. **Add threshold sensitivity analysis** — report the actual separation gap and discuss whether 1.5x is a phase transition or a post-hoc cutoff.\n\nIf the empirical results match projections and these revisions are made, the paper could reach 4-5/6.\n","numLines":224,"startLine":1,"totalLines":224}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014RiicTNrbHeJdjc6GhjYsk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both reviews. Let me conduct the roundtable process:\n\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing** - I now know what each reviewer said. Let me identify areas of agreement and disagreement.\n\n**Step 3: Convergence** - Identify consensus and remaining disagreements.\n\n**Step 4: Synthesize** - Write the final comprehensive review report.\n\nLet me now synthesize the roundtable discussion and produce the final report.\n\n## Roundtable Analysis\n\n### Areas of Strong Agreement:\n1. Both R1 and R2 rate 3/6 (Weak Reject) with confidence 4/5\n\n\n2. Both highlight the 008d vs 008f single-variable isolation as the paper's strongest element\n3. Both identify statistical underpowering (3 seeds insufficient) as a major concern\n4. Both note the scope overclaiming (title suggests generality but evidence is narrow)\n5. Both suggest scoping claims to RSSM-based models\n6. Both want at least one mitigation pilot experiment\n7. Both note the 1.5x threshold is post-hoc and not validated on held-out data\n8. Both give similar \"path to acceptance\" recommendations\n\n### R1-specific concerns:\n- Incomplete factorial design (missing cells in the 2x2x2 grid)\n- Need to run additional experiments to fill those gaps\n\n, potentially extending Cheetah runs to 200K+ steps to see if performance degradation turns into outright collapse\n- The nve metric has a degeneracy problem early in training where it conflates two separate failure modes: the value function becoming too optimistic versus actual returns dropping\n- Unclear whether the dt_embedding was trained then frozen or kept trainable\n\n### R2-specific concerns:\n- Related work is severely lacking with only 5 citations when there are roughly 13 critical papers missing from the discussion\n- Questions about data provenance—whether the multi-seed results actually come from separate experimental runs\n- The delusional policies concept is misattributed and should reference Lu et al. 2018\n- Title needs to be narrowed to RSSM-based models specifically\n- The core insight about how auxiliary conditioning plus model inaccuracy creates an exploitation channel deserves more emphasis\n- Missing discussion of conservative MBRL methods and multi-step value estimation approaches\n\nBoth reviewers are converging on what needs to happen for acceptance: running at least 5 seeds on the main experiments, substantially expanding the related work section, being more precise about what the claims actually apply to, including a mitigation experiment, and validating the threshold assumptions. The consensus lands at Weak Reject—the fundamental insight is sound and the 008d versus 008f comparison is well-executed, but the paper needs more statistical rigor, better coverage of prior work, tighter scoping, and fuller experimental validation before it's ready.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13260,"cache_read_input_tokens":60245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13260},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a90c8859-8e50-4bd7-a75e-c4f31c8dbc1b","uuid":"dc898d77-172d-4609-b866-287a65a34dfe"}
